      <abstract>&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A lightning talk about the Balloon Open Hardware License.&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA; </abstract>
      <description>&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The talk will be primarily about the Balloon Open Hardware&#xA;Licence on which we are currently solicting feedback. It will explain&#xA;why the licence is different from software Licences, distinguishing&#xA;between designers, manufacturers, and users, who needs different&#xA;rights and protections. We are actively seeking feedback on this&#xA;Licence, in order to make it widely suitable. A short history of the&#xA;Balloon Project and current status will be included.&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;i&gt;About the Balloon Project:&lt;/i&gt;&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Balloonboard is primarily about the design and use of the&#xA;Balloon series of open hardware boards. First Balloon hardware was in&#xA;2002, and Balloon2 was used in products of a couple of companies, for&#xA;Disabled speech sythesisers and seismometers. Balloon3 is now out,&#xA;with built-in FPGA, being used in University teaching (robotics) via&#xA;the Cambridge/MIT MDP.&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;We have created a new Open Hardware licence suitable for all such Open&#xA;Hardware, and are keen to see Balloon spread to other educational&#xA;institutions and products, and the licence be refined and used&#xA;elsewher.&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA; </description>
